      "content": "# X-01 Knowledge Base: Beginner Coding Reference\n### DEVCON Barangay AI Code Camp\n\nThis file is a plain-language reference for absolute beginners. Each section covers one topic in short, self-contained chunks so retrieval can pull the exact passage a question needs.\n\n---\n\n## HTML Basics\n\n**What HTML is.** HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the skeleton of a webpage. It doesn't make things look pretty — it just tells the browser what each piece of content *is*: a heading, a paragraph, a button, an image.\n\n**Basic structure.** Every HTML page starts with this shape:\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <title>My Page</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>Hello, World!</h1>\n    <p>This is a paragraph.</p>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n`<head>` holds page info (like the tab title). `<body>` holds everything visible on the page.\n\n**Common tags.**\n- `<h1>` to `<h6>` — headings, h1 is biggest/most important\n- `<p>` — paragraph of text\n- `<a href=\"...\">` — a clickable link\n- `<img src=\"...\" alt=\"...\">` — an image; `alt` describes it for screen readers\n- `<div>` — a generic container/box, used to group things\n- `<button>` — a clickable button\n- `<ul>` / `<li>` — a bulleted list and its items\n\n**Common beginner mistake:** forgetting to close a tag (e.g. `<p>Hello` with no `</p>`). Most tags need an opening and closing pair.\n\n---\n\n## CSS Basics\n\n**What CSS is.** CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) controls how HTML looks — colors, spacing, fonts, layout. HTML is the skeleton, CSS is the skin and clothes.\n\n**Three ways to add CSS:**\n1. Inline: `<p style=\"color: red;\">Hello</p>`\n2. Internal: a `<style>` block inside `<head>`\n3. External: a separate `.css` file linked with `<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">` (best practice)\n\n**Basic syntax:**\n```css\np {\n  color: blue;\n  font-size: 16px;\n}\n```\nThis says: \"every `<p>` element should have blue text at 16px.\"\n\n**Selectors:**\n- `p` — selects all `<p>` tags\n- `.classname` — selects elements with `class=\"classname\"`\n- `#idname` — selects the one element with `id=\"idname\"`\n\n**Box model.** Every HTML element is a box with, from inside out: content → padding → border → margin. Padding is space inside the border; margin is space outside it. This trips up almost every beginner at first.\n\n**Common beginner mistake:** confusing `margin` and `padding`, or forgetting that CSS selectors are case- and spelling-sensitive (`.Button` ≠ `.button`).\n\n---\n\n## JavaScript Basics\n\n**What JavaScript is.** JavaScript makes a webpage interactive — it reacts to clicks, updates content without reloading, and handles logic.\n\n**Variables:**\n```js\nlet name = \"Ana\";      // can change later\nconst age = 15;        // cannot be reassigned\n```\nUse `const` by default, `let` only when the value needs to change. Avoid `var` (older, less predictable).\n\n**Functions:**\n```js\nfunction greet(name) {\n  return \"Hello, \" + name + \"!\";\n}\n```\n\n**Conditionals:**\n```js\nif (age >= 13) {\n  console.log(\"Teen or older\");\n} else {\n  console.log(\"Younger than 13\");\n}\n```\n\n**Loops:**\n```js\nfor (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n  console.log(i);\n}\n```\n\n**Handling clicks (DOM basics):**\n```js\ndocument.getElementById(\"myButton\").addEventListener(\"click\", function() {\n  alert(\"Button clicked!\");\n});\n```\n\n**Common beginner mistake:** mismatched brackets/parentheses, forgetting a semicolon in the wrong place, or using `=` (assignment) when they mean `==`/`===` (comparison).\n\n---\n\n## Python Basics\n\n**What Python is.** Python is a beginner-friendly programming language known for readable syntax — no curly braces, indentation defines structure.\n\n**Variables:**\n```python\nname = \"Ana\"\nage = 15\n```\n\n**Conditionals:**\n```python\nif age >= 13:\n    print(\"Teen or older\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Younger than 13\")\n```\nIndentation (usually 4 spaces) is *required* in Python — it's not optional style, it defines which code belongs to the `if`.\n\n**Loops:**\n```python\nfor i in range(5):\n    print(i)\n```\n\n**Functions:**\n```python\ndef greet(name):\n    return f\"Hello, {name}!\"\n```\n\n**Lists:**\n```python\nfruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"mango\"]\nfruits.append(\"orange\")\n```\n\n**Common beginner mistake:** inconsistent indentation (mixing tabs and spaces), or forgetting the colon `:` after `if`, `for`, `def`, etc.\n\n---\n\n## Git & GitHub Basics\n\n**What Git is.** Git tracks changes to your code over time, like a save/undo history for a whole project. GitHub is a website that hosts Git projects online so others can see and collaborate on them.\n\n**Core workflow:**\n```bash\ngit init                     # start tracking a folder\ngit add .                    # stage all changed files\ngit commit -m \"message\"      # save a snapshot with a note\ngit push                     # upload your commits to GitHub\ngit pull                     # download the latest changes from GitHub\n```\n\n**Cloning an existing project:**\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/username/repo.git\n```\n\n**Branches (for later, once comfortable with the basics):**\n```bash\ngit branch new-feature       # create a branch\ngit checkout new-feature     # switch to it\n```\n\n**Common beginner mistake:** forgetting `git add` before `git commit` (nothing gets saved), or writing vague commit messages like \"update\" instead of describing what changed.\n\n---\n\n## General Debugging Strategies for Beginners\n\n1. **Read the error message first.** It usually tells you the exact line and what went wrong — don't skip past it.\n2. **Check for typos** in variable names, tag names, and brackets/parentheses/quotes — these cause the majority of beginner errors.\n3. **Comment things out.** If unsure what's breaking, disable half the code and see if the error goes away — narrows down the problem.\n4. **Print/log values.** Use `console.log()` (JS) or `print()` (Python) to check what a variable actually contains at each step.\n5. **Count your brackets.** Every `{`, `(`, `[` needs a matching closing partner.\n6. **Take a break.** A fresh look after 5 minutes often spots what tired eyes miss.\n"
